# Assistant RLM Dynamic Workflow Walkthrough

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"},
  {:runic_ai, path: "~/wrk/runic_ai"}
])

Code.require_file("support/runic_ai_livebook.exs", __DIR__)
```

## What This Notebook Teaches

This notebook explains one real assistant RLM run from the terminal harness:

```sh
mix runic_ai.agent \
  "Read /home/doops/wrk/runic_ai/tmp/wandering-inn-10-59-t.md and write a wiki-style chapter page
  in .docs with overview, events by actor, notable characters, plot points,
  reveals, and takeaways." \
  --agent assistant \
  --input tmp/wandering-inn-10-59-t.md \
  --output .docs/wandering-inn-10-59-t-summary.md \
  --allow-generated-functions tier1 \
  --write-policy docs-only \
  --detail expanded \
  --jsonl tmp/wandering-inn-10-59-t.assistant-rlm.jsonl \
  --max-runtime-ms 900000
```

The important idea is not "chapter summarization." The important idea is that
the assistant route treated the request as a workflow-generation problem:

* parse a terminal command into an objective, source, output, policy, and trace
  target
* generate an inert workflow blueprint as data
* validate graph, function-tier, and write-policy constraints
* compile the blueprint into trusted Runic components backed by anonymous
  functions and generic RLM components
* run bounded LLM calls over materialized slices instead of pushing the whole
  chapter into one prompt
* write a durable artifact and produce an evidence scorecard

This notebook reads the JSONL trace from the completed run, reconstructs the
workflow, and shows the prompts that were used by the generated LLM nodes.

## Load The Trace

The notebook is replaying evidence from the terminal harness. It does not make
another provider call.

```elixir
project_root = Path.expand("..", __DIR__)
trace_path = Path.join(project_root, "tmp/wandering-inn-10-59-t.assistant-rlm.jsonl")
artifact_path = Path.join(project_root, ".docs/wandering-inn-10-59-t-summary.md")

events =
  trace_path
  |> File.stream!()
  |> Stream.map(&String.trim/1)
  |> Stream.reject(&(&1 == ""))
  |> Enum.map(&Jason.decode!/1)

%{
  trace_path: Path.relative_to(trace_path, project_root),
  event_count: length(events),
  artifact_path: Path.relative_to(artifact_path, project_root),
  artifact_exists?: File.exists?(artifact_path)
}
```

```elixir
event_rows =
  Enum.map(events, fn event ->
    %{
      at: event["at"],
      phase: event["phase"],
      kind: event["kind"],
      status: event["status"],
      title: event["title"]
    }
  end)

Kino.DataTable.new(event_rows)
```

## Terminal Harness Path

`mix runic_ai.agent` is intentionally a presentation layer around backend
agents. The Mix task parses CLI options, opens the JSONL sink, starts the
assistant runner, and then renders normalized `RunicAI.Terminal.Event` values.

For this run:

* `--agent assistant` selected `RunicAI.Terminal.AssistantRunner`
* `--input` declared a source path for the generated workflow
* `--output` declared the artifact target
* `--allow-generated-functions tier1` allowed parameterized built-ins and the
  restricted expression interpreter, but no arbitrary Elixir source execution
* `--write-policy docs-only` restricted writes to `.docs`
* `--jsonl` made the run replayable by this notebook

```elixir
Kino.Mermaid.new("""
flowchart TD
  MixTask["Mix.Tasks.RunicAi.Agent<br/>parse argv"]
  Options["CliOptions<br/>agent/input/output/policy/jsonl"]
  Runner["AgentRunner<br/>presentation + JSONL sink"]
  AssistantRunner["AssistantRunner<br/>task wrapper + stage events"]
  Agent["RunicAI.Assistant.RLM.Agent"]
  Events["RunicAI.Terminal.Event<br/>presentation-neutral events"]
  Presenter["EventPresenter<br/>expanded terminal output"]
  JSONL["JsonlSink<br/>tmp/wandering-inn-10-59-t.assistant-rlm.jsonl"]

  MixTask --> Options
  Options --> Runner
  Runner --> AssistantRunner
  AssistantRunner --> Agent
  Agent -. "stage maps" .-> AssistantRunner
  AssistantRunner --> Events
  Events --> Presenter
  Events --> JSONL
""")
```

## What The Assistant Generated

The assistant route generated a workflow blueprint named
`assistant:generic_report`. In this first implementation the coordinator chose a
generic fallback shape because the command had a clear source file, a target
artifact, and a report-style objective. That choice is still dynamic workflow
generation: the workflow is built as data, validated, compiled, and executed for
this request. It is not a hard-coded chapter extractor.

```elixir
proposal_event = Enum.find(events, &(&1["phase"] == "proposed"))
proposal = proposal_event["data"]["proposal"]

node_rows =
  Enum.map(proposal["nodes"], fn node ->
    %{
      id: node["id"],
      kind: node["kind"],
      dependencies: Enum.join(node["dependencies"], ", "),
      params: inspect(node["params"], printable_limit: 180)
    }
  end)

Kino.Layout.tabs(
  "Blueprint nodes": Kino.DataTable.new(node_rows),
  "Blueprint data": Kino.Tree.new(proposal)
)
```

```elixir
Kino.Mermaid.new("""
flowchart LR
  Source["source_1<br/>tmp/wandering-inn-10-59-t.md"]
  Split["split_sources<br/>source_splitter<br/>paragraph split<br/>43 slices"]
  Materialize["materialize_slices<br/>source_materializer<br/>20 retained<br/>truncated: true"]
  Map["extract_slice_notes<br/>llm_map<br/>20 slice-note calls"]
  Synthesize["synthesize_report<br/>llm_leaf<br/>hierarchical reduce"]
  Write["write_artifact<br/>artifact_writer<br/>.docs/wandering-inn-10-59-t-summary.md"]
  Verify["verify_objective<br/>verification<br/>objective_satisfied"]

  Source --> Split
  Split --> Materialize
  Materialize --> Map
  Map --> Synthesize
  Synthesize --> Write
  Write --> Verify
""")
```

## Why This Workflow Shape

The objective was a long-document synthesis task with a durable artifact target.
A direct prompt would have poor auditability and could hit context limits. The
generated graph uses a safer decomposition:

```elixir
Kino.DataTable.new([
  %{
    pressure: "Long source document",
    workflow_decision: "split_sources",
    reason: "Create bounded source slices with provenance instead of one giant prompt."
  },
  %{
    pressure: "Model context and cost limits",
    workflow_decision: "materialize_slices",
    reason: "Cap slice bytes and total bytes before LLM calls."
  },
  %{
    pressure: "Need coherent coverage",
    workflow_decision: "extract_slice_notes",
    reason: "Ask the model for useful notes per slice, keeping each call bounded."
  },
  %{
    pressure: "Need one wiki-style page",
    workflow_decision: "synthesize_report",
    reason: "Reduce mapped notes into a single report rather than concatenating raw slice output."
  },
  %{
    pressure: "Durable deliverable",
    workflow_decision: "write_artifact",
    reason: "Write through workspace path policy using docs-only mutation."
  },
  %{
    pressure: "Evidence of completion",
    workflow_decision: "verify_objective",
    reason: "Check that the requested artifact exists and record an objective scorecard."
  }
])
```

## Architecture Concepts

RunicAI separates the generated plan from the trusted runtime. The model or
coordinator can propose data, but trusted Elixir code owns validation,
compilation, file access, and LLM invocation.

```elixir
Kino.Mermaid.new("""
flowchart TD
  Objective["Objective + sources + policies"]
  Manifest["Capability manifest<br/>component types<br/>function tiers<br/>write policy<br/>budgets"]
  Blueprint["WorkflowBlueprint<br/>inert data"]
  Components["ComponentBlueprints<br/>type + params + edges"]
  Functions["FunctionBlueprints<br/>Tier 0 built-ins<br/>Tier 1 expressions"]
  Validator["BlueprintValidator<br/>graph + policy checks"]
  Compiler["WorkflowCompiler<br/>trusted closure construction"]
  Runic["Runic workflow graph"]
  Runtime["Sequential execution in this run<br/>events + episodes + artifacts"]

  Objective --> Manifest
  Manifest --> Blueprint
  Blueprint --> Components
  Blueprint --> Functions
  Components --> Validator
  Functions --> Validator
  Validator --> Compiler
  Compiler --> Runic
  Runic --> Runtime
""")
```

The safety boundary is the key design point:

* Tier 0: parameterized built-ins such as `join`, `get`, `template`, `dedupe_by`
* Tier 1: a restricted expression language interpreted by trusted code
* Tier 2: arbitrary Elixir function blueprints are not executed by this path
  unless a future review-gated validator accepts them
* writes are routed through workspace path policy, and this run only allowed
  `.docs`

## The Prompts In The Generated Workflow

The run produced three prompt templates in the generated blueprint:

* per-slice map prompt
* group-reduce prompt
* final synthesis prompt

```elixir
node_by_id = Map.new(proposal["nodes"], &{&1["id"], &1})

map_prompt = node_by_id["extract_slice_notes"]["params"]["prompt"]
group_prompt = node_by_id["synthesize_report"]["params"]["group_prompt"]
final_prompt = node_by_id["synthesize_report"]["params"]["prompt"]

prompt_markdown = fn prompt ->
  prompt
  |> String.split("\n")
  |> Enum.map(&("    " <> &1))
  |> Enum.join("\n")
  |> Kino.Markdown.new()
end

Kino.Layout.tabs(
  "Map prompt": prompt_markdown.(map_prompt),
  "Group reduce prompt": prompt_markdown.(group_prompt),
  "Final synthesis prompt": prompt_markdown.(final_prompt)
)
```

The map prompt is deliberately generic. It does not know about this chapter's
characters or plot taxonomy. It asks for concrete facts, actors, entities,
decisions, claims, evidence, uncertainties, and source references because those
are broadly useful for many assistant report tasks.

The final prompt also stays generic. The requested sections came from the user
objective, so the model could shape the final wiki page around overview, events,
characters, plot points, reveals, and takeaways without RunicAI shipping a
fiction-specific extractor module.

## Runtime Evidence From The Trace

The JSONL trace includes both live stage events and final result artifacts. This
is enough to reconstruct the run after the fact.

```elixir
node_completed =
  events
  |> Enum.filter(&(&1["phase"] == "node_completed"))
  |> Enum.map(fn event ->
    data = event["data"]["data"]
    output = data["output"] || %{}

    %{
      at: event["at"],
      node_id: data["node_id"],
      type: data["type"],
      status: event["status"],
      output: inspect(output, printable_limit: 220)
    }
  end)

Kino.DataTable.new(node_completed)
```

```elixir
scorecard_event = Enum.find(events, &(&1["phase"] == "scorecard"))
scorecard = scorecard_event["data"]["scorecard"]

Kino.Tree.new(scorecard)
```

## The Long-Document Map/Reduce Shape

The source splitter found 43 paragraph slices. The materializer retained 20
slices under the configured byte budget. Those 20 slices became 20 LLM map
calls. The reduce node used `max_group_items: 12`, so the effective reduce shape
was two group reductions followed by one final synthesis.

```elixir
Kino.Mermaid.new("""
flowchart TD
  Chapter["Long chapter file"]
  Split["paragraph split<br/>43 slices"]
  Materialize["materialize budget<br/>20 retained<br/>truncated: true"]

  subgraph MapPhase["LLM map phase"]
    S1["slice 1"]
    S2["slice 2"]
    SMid["..."]
    S20["slice 20"]
  end

  Notes["20 slice-note outputs"]

  subgraph ReducePhase["LLM reduce phase"]
    G1["group reduce<br/>items 1-12"]
    G2["group reduce<br/>items 13-20"]
    Final["final synthesis<br/>wiki-style page"]
  end

  Artifact[".docs/wandering-inn-10-59-t-summary.md<br/>15,240 bytes"]

  Chapter --> Split --> Materialize
  Materialize --> S1
  Materialize --> S2
  Materialize --> SMid
  Materialize --> S20
  S1 --> Notes
  S2 --> Notes
  SMid --> Notes
  S20 --> Notes
  Notes --> G1
  Notes --> G2
  G1 --> Final
  G2 --> Final
  Final --> Artifact
""")
```

## Timeline

Most of the runtime was in the model-backed map and reduce calls. Splitting,
materialization, writing, and verification were local and effectively
instantaneous.

```elixir
Kino.Mermaid.new("""
gantt
  title Assistant RLM Run Timing From JSONL Trace
  dateFormat HH:mm:ss
  axisFormat %H:%M:%S

  section Setup
  Terminal start and manifest       :done, setup, 17:12:06, 1s
  Blueprint proposal and validation :done, plan, 17:12:06, 1s

  section Workflow Nodes
  split_sources                     :done, split, 17:12:06, 1s
  materialize_slices                :done, materialize, 17:12:06, 1s
  extract_slice_notes               :done, map, 17:12:06, 17:16:09
  synthesize_report                 :done, reduce, 17:16:09, 17:17:31
  write_artifact                    :done, write, 17:17:31, 1s
  verify_objective                  :done, verify, 17:17:31, 1s
""")
```

